<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Koknese</h1><p class="st-meta">1180 CE – 1208 CE (approx.) · capital Koknese</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Orthodox principality on the Daugava, vassal of Polotsk, swallowed by the crusading Bishopric of Riga in 1208.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Koknese, called Kukenois by the Germans, was a fortified centre on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Latvia</a>, inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalians</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/selonians">Selonians</a>. By the late 12th century it was ruled by a prince of Rus' origin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyachko">Vyachko</a>, as a tributary of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-polotsk">Principality of Polotsk</a>. It is known almost entirely from the chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-livonia">Henry of Livonia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality controlled a stretch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and the Baltic and collected tribute from the surrounding Baltic peoples. Its prince and his retinue were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christians, while most of the population remained pagan. When German crusaders founded Riga in 1201, Koknese came under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Bishop Albert</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1207 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyachko">Vyachko</a> gave half his land to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in return for protection. Quarrels with German knights followed, and in 1208 he burned his castle and fled to Rus'. The bishop built a stone castle on the site, and Vyachko died defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a> against the crusaders in 1224.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-koknese.jpg" alt="Principality of Koknese" width="320" height="164"><figcaption>Image: Garais · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koknese.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1180 CE – 1208 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Koknese</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vyachko</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latgalian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kukenois, Koknese, Kokenhusen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1194&amp;focus=principality-of-koknese" aria-label="Principality of Koknese on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5230 253 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Koknese lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5550 509L5630 540M5550 509L5520 501"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5630 540h0M5520 501h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5550 509h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5550 509h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Koknese. <a href="/atlas?year=1194&amp;focus=principality-of-koknese">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

